
page object in AlphaByNod

type object  -- virtual, restricted
{{
	This is a special virtual type definition. 
	
	This definition also serves as a domain for essential
	methods.
	
	Essential methods and virtual type object have special 
	status in Nod.  
	
	Conceptually, type object is a virtual base type from
	which all other types derive, while essential methods	
	automatically belong to every type definition (includ-
	ing this one).
	
	Technically, essential methods aren't inherited from
	type object.  Rather, they're "included" by the com-
	piler, as if they were written explicitly in the type
	definition.

	:null? and :not_null? assign an output consistent with
	imo null state.
	
	:renew terminates imo and prepares it for initializat-
	ion. imo is null after calling :renew.

	:can_upd? returns true if imo doesn't have a runtime
	[eval] constraint.

	:can_init? returns true if imo doesn't have a runtime
	constraint.  If can_init? is true, imo is an original
	type.


	:is<t>? returns true if imo actual type	derives from <t>.
	
	:is_actual<t>? returns true if imo actual type is <t>.
	
	:as_base<t> is a base sub-object translator with re-
	served permission syntax. It never returns a void proxy
	because	type is verified at compile time. 
	
	Permission is reduced to [eval,init] if the lead object 
	type is typical, or [eval,upd] otherwise. Then the re-
	duced dual permission is resolved in the usual way.  

	Note: This allows base type initialization within a
	typical method, but prevents it otherwise. :as_base<t>
	won't won't fail from runtime verification of permission.

	:as<t> is a random sub-object translator. It returns a 
	transient proxy if imo actual type is <t> or derives 
	from <t>. Otherwise the result is void.

	A transient proxy must be authorized before it can be
	used to update or initialize it's joined object. 
	Typically that's accomplished by specifying an overt 
	permission in the call expression. However, if the 
	result is void, runtime authorization will cause a 
	disruption.
	
	x:as<thing>[upd] -- disruption if actual type of x 
					 -- doesn't derive from thing
	
	The application shouldn't depend on a void :as<t> result
	to test object type when it also specifies an overt 
	permission in the call. If an overt permission is not 
	specified, the result can be tested for void, but it's
	still transient and can	only be used to evaluate it's 
	joined object. 

	Best practice is to pretest object type using :is<t>?
	if :as<t> might return a void result.

	:as<t> cannot be used to increase access to an object.

	If the lead object permission is known (not transient), 
	the compiler verifies that overt permission doesn't
	increase access. Furthermore, if the overt permission 
	is [init] and doesn't increase access, the lead object
	permission must also be [init], which means <t> and 
	lead object type are both actual types, which means 
	:as<t>[init] is extraneous.
	
	The only time :as<t>[init] is useful is when the lead 
	object is a transient proxy. In that case, permission
	is verified at runtime. Thus, if :as<t>[init] doesn't
	disrupt, the result proxy is good.

	Calling :as<t> without an overt proxy spec, removes
	any known lead object permission and makes the result
	transient.

	Note:  Generic methods use reserved [app] syntax to 
	override standard lean calling convention.
}}
{
	general 
	{
		null? ()( bool );

		not_null? ()( bool );

		renew [term,app];	 
		

		get_typeid ()( typeid );	-- copy

		get_addr ()( addr );		-- copy


		is<t>? [app]()( bool );  

		is_actual<t>? [app]()( bool ); 


		as_base<t> = <t> prox [eval,upd,init]; -- compile-time

		as<t> [app] = <t> prox;	-- transient, may be void

	}
}